A||||18||sqoach||What to do next?||||1||
Z||000000||sqoach||05-10-2001||08:06 PM||||Ok, seems as though everything I've tried to fix my overheating isn't working. So, just about the only thing left to do is take my engine apart and check for cracks and warping in the head, and replace the head gasket of course. [I've checked everything I can think of, and what everyone else has thought of too, so please don't ask the obvious if you must ask what I've checked  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">]<P>I guess my question is, should I just do a partial rebuild down to the head, or go for a full rebuild? I really don't want to spend any more money than I have to. I do plan on selling my car whenever I get this overheating resolved, because I doubt it would sell very good the way it is.<P>Any suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||05-10-2001||09:41 PM||||Sqoach: I know you've tried just about everything, but I've never heard you try a combustion leak tester yet(I may have missed that thread though). It detects CO(a byproduct of combustion) in the coolant. The only way the CO can get in the coolant is if a gasket is leaking. They typically cost $30 or so.||||reg||1
Z||000002||c m west||05-10-2001||11:20 PM||||did you try looking for bent or crushed coolant tubes?||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||05-11-2001||10:29 AM||||fiero56, no, I haven't tried that yet. Probably because I've never heard of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>I'm pretty sure that's what's happening though. Somehow, air or something is getting into the water system and causing my "overheating."<BR>Of course if that test shows I have a leaking gasket, I'm going to have to take apart the engine anyway, right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>c m west, yes I've checked for that. All is good.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-11-2001||01:12 PM||||Don't know if you've replaced your water pump, or not.<BR>Mine started leaking through the weep hole, behind the pulley. The only way you'll be able to tell if it's leaking there, is by a puddle under the car after it's been parked for a while. I've also seen coolant leak from the plugs in the metal tubes, under the doos. The plugs are at the back of the tubes, about a foot in front of the back wheel, on both sides of the car. <BR>If you've checked all of this before, please excuse me repeating.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||05-11-2001||03:30 PM||||Yep, replaced the water pump. There are no external leaks anywhere.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||05-11-2001||04:59 PM||||I looked over your past posts about this problem, or least what I could find. I noticed you replaced the radiator cap. Did you actually replace the radiator cap, or the filler cap? If you replaced the actual radiator cap, what was the pressure rating? It needs to be around 12 to 14 psi venting. You might try putting a non-venting one on as a test to see what happens, but I wouldn't suggest leaving it there. It occured to me you may have a weak hose that's expanding, and relieving pressure in the system, causing the coolant to boil.<BR>I also didn't see anything about the fan circuit, if it was checked thoroughly. And if so, what was determined. Or specifying when exactly it overheats; while you're driving, while you're in traffic, or after you shut it off.<BR>If I understand, you say it boils over. If it boils, then it's not holding pressure. Pressure in the system is all that keeps it from boiling normally. Pressure on a fluid increases it's boiling point. So if it's boiling over, then it's because the system isn't holding pressure. (thus my idea about the weak hose...while it would maintain a certain amount of pressure, maybe not enough to prevent boiling)<BR>How about maybe a quick, yet thorough rundown of everything you've done, and the results of any checks or tests you've done.  (This is the problem with starting new threads for the same problem....too difficult to keep up with what's been done and said)<BR>I highly doubt your problem is a cracked head or damaged gasket. If either of those conditions exist, usually you'll get a significant amount of steam from the exhaust, and the system boils over from lack (or loss) of water. (that was what happend to my 84 Duck)And if the crack or leak is small enough that no steam would be noticed, then it's not big enough to cause overheating.<BR>There are a bunch of things left to be loooked at I think, that are much more likely than a cracked head. Put the list here so we can all see what you've done, and the result or outcome, and maybe try to keep everything in the future limited to this one thread, so they don't get lost in the shuffle. <BR>~Taiji<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||05-11-2001||05:01 PM||||I have a few ?'s 4 U.<P>How long ago and what were your symptoms for the pump replacement?<P>When you over-heat now, is it a huge boil over or just a light and loads of temperature?<P>I may have something you should try before you go for a top end tear down. It just depends on your answers.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tryxalon||05-11-2001||06:13 PM||||Did you ever correct your coolant mixture? (earlier post said you were using more water than antifreeze). <P>The 50/50 water/antifreeze mix is a neccessity for the system to work right - along with a sealed and pressurized system. <P>a 60/40 water antifreeze mix will boil at 195F at normal pressure.  A 50/50 mix under 14 pounds of pressure will not boil until 290F (or so).   MAKES A BIG difference. <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||sqoach||05-11-2001||10:48 PM||||Ok, let's see if I can get everything in this post. It's gonna be pretty long, so bear with me here.<P>Things I have replaced/checked:<BR>-water pump<BR>-thermostat (180 from rodney)<BR>-fan switch (195 from rodney)<BR>-fan motor<BR>-fan relay<BR>-radiator cap (Stant 16lb non-vented)<BR>-thermostat housing cap<BR>-both belts are tight and are less than a few months old<BR>-radiator's been rodded out<BR>-checked all coolant lines-none of them are crushed-hoses looked fine<BR>-no external leaks anywhere<BR>-no oil in the coolant<BR>-no coolant in the oil<P>I can remember the first time my car overheated. It was almost a year ago when me and some friends went camping. They told me antifreeze was leaking from my car. Turns out the fan wasn't kicking on. It ran ok when I was moving, but warmed up when just idling. So I made it home ok running a little warm. That's when I replaced all the fan related stuff. From then on it's kinda blurry. I don't remember having a problem with it that fall. It was in november when it was a common thing for it to overheat, and that's when I signed up to this forum, asking for help.<P>Now I'm gonna try to describe what happens when my car "overheats"<BR>My car will do ok for a few days. (I usually drive it everyday) Then, just out of the blue, the temp starts to go up more than normal. Then it sinks back down, then up again and stays up. When I notice it start to go up, that's when I know it's time to take it home. I haven't let it boil over in a while cuz I know what to watch for now. I get it home as soon as I can and the overflow tank is nearly full. I let it cool for 30 minutes to an hour, then I drain some of the coolant from the overflow tank, "burp" the system, and I'm on my merry way. Few days later...you guessed it. This is the life of me and my car.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||05-11-2001||11:27 PM||||You have a 2.5 in your coupe, correct?<BR>When you replaced the water pump, did you install a gasket? If you did, remove you pump and gasket, use a file and remove any burs from the water pump mating surfaces and reinstall it with silicon gasket sealer only. Let it (the silicon) cure as per instructions on tube and refill the system.<BR>There have been many instances that I've found that there is too much room between the impeller and the pump housing and the pump will not move enough coolant. If you had a leaking head gasket, 9 times out of 10 you will build pressure way before the engine reaches full operating temps and will not wait for every 'few' days to happen. I haven't used 2.5 water pump gaskets for a loooong time! Give it a shot, it's tons cheaper than a top end that may not fix it any way.||||reg||1
Z||000011||sqoach||05-12-2001||01:14 AM||||Yep, good 'ol iron duke 2.5  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yes, I used the gasket that came with the water pump and I used gasket sealant on both sides of it. How could that make my car "overheat" every few days? It runs perfectly fine and cool on the "good days"<P>I might try it for the hell of it if I get bored next week or even tomorrow. I just remember replacing that thing was kinda a pain in the a$$.||||reg||1
Z||000012||sqoach||05-12-2001||01:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>If you had a leaking head gasket, 9 times out of 10 you will build pressure way before the engine reaches full operating temps and will not wait for every 'few' days to happen.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Would it be possible to build a little pressure everytime I drive my car? Then finally there's enough to cause a false overheat?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Taijiguy||05-12-2001||04:25 AM||||Check to see if there's alot of moisture or steam in the exhaust. That sounds alot like what my Duck was doing. I never tore into it to fix it, but I knew it was a bad gasket or head. It sounds like it's consuming coolant, the level drops, and it overheats. <BR>Do you keep a pretty constant eye on the water level...like check it every day..or do you fill it, then check it when it overheats again? Try "burping" or topping off the system every day for a while, and see if it stops overheating. All this does is confirm that the motor is actually burning coolant.<BR>~Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GTZ34||05-12-2001||09:13 AM||||Sqoach,  If you are seeping into a cylinder a little at a time, then the plugs should show a non-normal burn color.  I guess it would be possible that when the piston is in the intake stroke, the vacuum could pull in slight seepage if you had a leak somewhere top end.  I would definitely think that the plugs and carbonization in the head would show it.<P>GL||||reg||1
Z||000015||Indiana_resto_guy||05-12-2001||09:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B> Would it be possible to build a little pressure every time I drive my car? Then finally there's enough to cause a false overheat?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the compression stroke (approx. 100 lbs. PSI. each cylinder), detonation forces (along with the heat generated) and engine temp. are taken into consideration, the head gasket should have been burnt and blown through any weak spot long ago. In comparison with the cooling systems relief pressure of your radiators cap (16 lbs. PSI), it wouldn't take long to build enough pressure to generate a 'boil over condition'.<P>Other ?'s 4 U.<P>When it over heats, have you been doing a lot of slow speed driving or sitting at idle for any period of time?<BR>Do you have A/C (need not be functioning to cooling interior). If you do turn it on to start the fan when your temp rises. If this averts a boil over you don't have a blown head gasket as this small test will not do anything with compressed air in the system from a blown gasket. What it will prove is that there is poor circulation within the cooling system.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||sqoach||05-12-2001||11:57 AM||||Taij, ever since I can remember, my car has smoked a little more than it's suppose to from the tailpipe. Kind of an off-white color to it. Maybe even greyish. And there always seems to be some water droplets, sometimes quite a bit. I could rev my engine, and there would be this small spray of water. That was leading me to believe I was losing coolant. But my dad told me it's normal to have some water/condensation coming from the exhaust.<BR>I try to keep a close eye on my coolant level. From what I've examined, it seems like the bottle fills up after I drive my car (yeah I know that's normal) but the level doesn't go down all the way to where it was the time before. And I've let it sit for quite some time. The clamp on the hose from the radiator to the bottle is tight, too.<P>87, I'll check my plugs when I do another compression check. Seems like one of the spark plugs looked a little strange last time.<P>resto, I always drive around town, and there's nothing I would call traffic in this town. Speed limits between 25-45 mph. And I never just sit there and idle. Unless I'm at a stoplight, but that's not even a minute. And yes, I have A/C. When I turn it on, the fan kicks on like it's suppose to. I also have a new fan switch that kicks on when it gets hot enough.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Taijiguy||05-12-2001||02:02 PM||||I would pull the filler cap to check the level, not just eyeball the overflow tank. It's only effective to the point that coolant enters and leaves that tank. If water is being "burned" it won't create a vacuum in the system to draw the coolant from the overflow. Check the water at the filler. That will give you a much more accurate indication of what's happening.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||sqoach||05-13-2001||07:11 PM||||But if the overflow bottle is fuller (is that a word?) than it was, then most likely the level at the stat housing will be lower. And I just went to check both. Overflow bottle has more, stat housing has less.<P>I'm kind of the middle man in this deal. I take the coolant from the overflow bottle that's suppose to be sucked back into the water system. Then I get the air out and put the coolant back in. That's the only way I can describe it. I guess it's just not sucking back in because air that has somehow gotten in has taken its place.||||reg||1
